\begin{abstract}
We study a proposal to build a number system on the M\"obius band
$M = ([0,1]\times\R)/\big((0,y)\sim(1,-y)\big)$, in which the point $(x,y)$ represents the
number $y(2x-1)$ and the integers are embedded by $n\mapsto\big(\tfrac12+\tfrac1{2n},|n|\big)$.
The proposal predicts that the image $\Z_M$ carries a ring structure induced from
coordinatewise operations, that $1$ and $-1$ are identified at the seam so that $\Z_M$ is a
one-point compactification of $\Z$, that $(1,0)\cdot(0,1)=(0,0)$ exhibits zero divisors, and
that orientation appears as a ``twist prime''.
We establish one confirmation and four refutations. The value map $\val(x,y)=y(2x-1)$ \emph{is}
well defined on $M$ --- it is a section of the twisted line bundle --- and the seam
identification $[(0,-1)]=[(1,1)]$ is genuine. However, neither coordinatewise addition nor
coordinatewise multiplication descends to $M$; the value of the embedded integer $n$ is exactly
$\sgn(n)$; the embedding is injective with unbounded image, so $\pm1$ are not identified and
$\Z_M$ is not compact; and the alleged nonzero factor $(1,0)$ equals $0$ in $M$.
We then relocate the surviving intuition into the \emph{twist ring} $\Ztw=\Z[t]/(t^2-1)$,
realised as $\{(u,v)\in\Z^2 : u\equiv v \bmod 2\}$ via $a+bt\mapsto(a+b,a-b)$. We prove that the
norm $N(a+bt)=a^2-b^2$ is multiplicative and detects units, that the unit group is
$\{\pm1,\pm t\}\cong(\Z/2)^2$ so the twist is a unit and never prime, that $z$ is a zero divisor
iff $N(z)=0$ iff $a=\pm b$ (the algebraic image of the seam), that no element has norm $\pm2$
whence $2$ is irreducible while every odd integer $2k+1=((k+1)+kt)((k+1)-kt)$ splits, giving the
classification ``$n\in\Z$ is irreducible in $\Ztw$ iff $n=\pm2$'', and that $\Ztw$ has no
nontrivial idempotents, hence $\Ztw\not\cong\Z\times\Z$ despite $t^2=1$. Finally, periodic and
antiperiodic functions form a $\Z/2$-graded system whose holonomy is computed by powers of $t$,
and every continuous antiperiodic function vanishes in every window of length one, so the odd
part contains no units. Orientation is a grading, acting by units, and not a prime.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} M\"obius band, non-orientability, twist ring, group ring
$\Z[\Z/2]$, hyperbolic norm form, zero divisors, irreducibility, antiperiodic functions,
holonomy.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The proposal}

The M\"obius band is the simplest non-orientable surface, and its defining feature is a holonomy
of order two: transport an orientation once around the core circle and it reverses, twice and it
returns. Signs in arithmetic behave the same way. It is therefore natural to ask whether one can
\emph{identify} the two phenomena --- build a number system on the M\"obius band in which the
geometric twist becomes the arithmetic minus sign, and in which orientation functions as an
irreducible element, a ``twist prime''.

Concretely, the proposal we test is the following.

\paragraph{Model.} Let $M$ be the quotient of $\R\times\R$ by the relation generated by
$(0,y)\sim(1,-y)$ (points off the seam are identified with themselves only).

\paragraph{Value.} The point $(x,y)$ is to represent $\val(x,y)=y(2x-1)$.

\paragraph{M\"obius integers.} $\Z_M$ is the image of
$\emb(n)=\big(\tfrac12+\tfrac1{2n},\,|n|\big)$, $n\in\Z$.

\paragraph{Claims.}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(C\arabic*)]
\item $\val$ descends to $M$.
\item $\Z_M$ is a ring under the operations induced from coordinatewise $+$ and $\cdot$.
\item $1$ and $-1$ are identified at the twist point, making $\Z_M$ a one-point compactification
of $\Z$.
\item $\Z_M$ is not a domain, because $(1,0)\cdot(0,1)=(0,0)$ with neither factor zero.
\item Every nonzero M\"obius integer factors as $\pm p_1^{a_1}\cdots p_k^{a_k}$ with the overall
sign being a ``twist prime''; e.g. $6=2_+3_+$ and $-6=2_+3_+\cdot(-1)$.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Results and organising principles}

Section~\ref{sec:band} confirms (C1) and refutes (C2)--(C4) and the premise of (C5).
Section~\ref{sec:ring} constructs the twist ring and proves a complete
unit/irreducibility/zero-divisor analysis, including the corrected factorisation test.
Section~\ref{sec:sections} links the algebra to sections of the M\"obius line bundle.
Section~\ref{sec:algo} gives algorithms; Sections~\ref{sec:apps}--\ref{sec:future} discuss
applications, limitations, and future work. Three principles organise the paper.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Twist = grading, not sign, not prime.} What (C5) wanted is realised by a
degree-one element $t$ with $t^2=1$, necessarily a \emph{unit}. Grading groups act by units.
\item \textbf{The zero-divisor locus is the seam.} In $\Ztw$, $z$ is a zero divisor iff
$N(z)=0$, i.e. $a=\pm b$: two lines, the algebraic image of $(0,y)\sim(1,-y)$.
\item \textbf{Splitting is governed by $a^2-b^2$.} Odd integers split, $2$ stays irreducible, no
element has norm $\pm2$: the arithmetic of the hyperbolic form, not of a quadratic field.
\end{enumerate}

\section{The M\"obius band and the proposed number system}\label{sec:band}

\subsection{The band as a quotient}

\begin{definition}[Seam relation]
For $p,q\in\R\times\R$ declare $p\mathrel{R}q$ iff $p=q$, or $(p_1=0,\,q_1=1,\,q_2=-p_2)$, or
$(p_1=1,\,q_1=0,\,q_2=-p_2)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$R$ is an equivalence relation.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Reflexivity is the first clause; symmetry swaps the seam clauses and uses $-(-y)=y$. For
transitivity, the only nonvacuous composite of two seam moves returns to the original point,
since $(0,y)\mapsto(1,-y)\mapsto(0,y)$; the mixed composites are impossible because $0\ne1$.
\end{proof}

Write $M=(\R\times\R)/R$ and $[p]$ for the class of $p$, so $[p]=[q]$ iff $p\mathrel{R}q$. The
\emph{seam} is the image of $\{x\in\{0,1\}\}$; the \emph{core} is the image of $\{y=0\}$.

\subsection{Confirmed: the value map descends}

\begin{definition}
$\val(x,y):=y(2x-1)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The value map is well defined]\label{thm:val}
If $p\mathrel{R}q$ then $\val(p)=\val(q)$; hence there is a unique
$\overline{\val}:M\to\R$ with $\overline{\val}([p])=\val(p)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $p=(0,y)$, $q=(1,-y)$ we get $\val(p)=y(2\cdot0-1)=-y$ and $\val(q)=(-y)(2\cdot1-1)=-y$; the
other clause is symmetric.
\end{proof}

This says precisely that $x\mapsto 2x-1$ is a section of the M\"obius line bundle: a fibrewise
linear rule reversing sign across the seam, compensating the reversal of the fibre coordinate.

\begin{theorem}[Twist point]
$[(0,-1)]=[(1,1)]$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $p=(0,-1)$, $q=(1,1)$: then $p_1=0$, $q_1=1$, $q_2=1=-(-1)=-p_2$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Refuted: no induced ring operations}

\begin{theorem}[No induced addition]\label{thm:noadd}
There is no map $f:M\times M\to M$ with $f([p],[q])=[p+q]$ for all $p,q$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$[(0,1)]=[(1,-1)]$ by the seam clause, so such $f$ would give
$[(0,1)+(0,1)]=[(1,-1)+(1,-1)]$, i.e. $[(0,2)]=[(2,-2)]$. The points differ, and no seam clause
applies since $2\notin\{0,1\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No induced multiplication]\label{thm:nomul}
There is no map $f:M\times M\to M$ with $f([p],[q])=[p\cdot q]$ (coordinatewise product).
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Existence forces $[(0,1)\cdot(0,1)]=[(1,-1)\cdot(1,-1)]$, i.e. $[(0,1)]=[(1,1)]$. These differ,
and the seam clause relating first coordinates $0$ and $1$ demands opposite fibre coordinates,
whereas both are $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
These failures are structural. The gluing is \emph{fibrewise linear}: it identifies the fibre
over $0$ with the fibre over $1$ by $y\mapsto-y$ and says nothing about the base. Coordinatewise
operations mix base coordinates invisibly to the quotient. Any algebra on $M$ must be fibrewise
--- which is exactly what a line bundle supports (Section~\ref{sec:sections}).
\end{remark}

\subsection{Refuted: the value map collapses $\Z$}

\begin{theorem}[The embedding records only the sign]\label{thm:valemb}
For all $n\in\Z$, $\val(\emb(n))=\sgn(n)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $n\ne0$,
$\val(\emb(n))=|n|\big(2(\tfrac12+\tfrac1{2n})-1\big)=|n|/n$, which is $1$ for $n>0$ and $-1$
for $n<0$; for $n=0$ the fibre coordinate is $0$, so the value is $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Collapse]
$\overline{\val}([\emb(2)])=\overline{\val}([\emb(3)])=1$: the proposed number system cannot
distinguish $2$ from $3$.
\end{corollary}

The magnitude $|n|$ placed in the fibre is cancelled exactly by the $1/(2n)$ placed in the base.

\subsection{Refuted: no identification, no compactification}

\begin{lemma}[Seam detection]\label{lem:seam}
$(\emb n)_1=0\iff n=-1$ and $(\emb n)_1=1\iff n=1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Injectivity in the plane]\label{lem:injplane}
$\emb(m)=\emb(n)\iff m=n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Second coordinates give $|m|=|n|$; for nonzero arguments the first coordinates give $1/m=1/n$;
and $|m|=|n|=0$ forces $m=n=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$1$ and $-1$ are not identified]
$[\emb(1)]\ne[\emb(-1)]$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\emb(1)=(1,1)$ and $\emb(-1)=(0,1)$ are distinct, so equality in $M$ needs a seam clause; that
clause demands opposite fibre coordinates, but both equal $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Injectivity into the band]
$n\mapsto[\emb(n)]$ is injective $\Z\to M$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $[\emb(m)]=[\emb(n)]$ then either $\emb(m)=\emb(n)$ and $m=n$ by
Lemma~\ref{lem:injplane}, or a seam clause applies, which by Lemma~\ref{lem:seam} forces
$\{m,n\}=\{1,-1\}$ and then $1=-1$, absurd.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unboundedness, hence non-compactness]
For every $C$ there is $n$ with $\|\emb(n)\|>C$; hence $\{\emb(n):n\in\Z\}$ is not compact.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Pick a natural $k>\max(C,0)$; the fibre coordinate of $\emb(k)$ is $k>C$, and compact subsets of
$\R^2$ are bounded.
\end{proof}

Thus $\Z_M\cong\Z$ as a set, with no point at infinity: the twist, which reverses the fibre
coordinate, is exactly what keeps $\pm1$ apart, because $\emb$ makes both fibre coordinates
equal to $|n|>0$.

\subsection{Refuted: the zero-divisor witness}

\begin{theorem}
$[(1,0)]=[(0,0)]$, and $(1,0)$ is not a M\"obius integer.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For the first claim take $p=(1,0)$, $q=(0,0)$: $p_1=1$, $q_1=0$, $q_2=0=-0=-p_2$. For the
second, $|n|=0$ forces $n=0$, whose first coordinate is $\tfrac12\ne1$.
\end{proof}

Hence $(1,0)\cdot(0,1)=(0,0)$ exhibits no zero divisor: one factor is the zero of the band.
Geometrically, the seam pinches the zero section into the core circle, on which $(1,0)$ lies.

\section{The twist ring $\Ztw=\Z[t]/(t^2-1)$}\label{sec:ring}

\subsection{Definition and a concrete model}

\begin{definition}
$\Ztw:=\Z[t]/(t^2-1)$, with elements $a+bt$ ($a,b\in\Z$) and
\[
(a+bt)+(c+dt)=(a+c)+(b+d)t,\qquad (a+bt)(c+dt)=(ac+bd)+(ad+bc)t .
\]
This is the integral group ring $\Z[\Z/2]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Character coordinates]
Let $\chi_\pm:\Ztw\to\Z$ be the ring maps with $t\mapsto\pm1$, and
$\Phi(a+bt):=(\chi_+(a+bt),\chi_-(a+bt))=(a+b,\,a-b)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Model]\label{prop:model}
$\Phi$ is an injective ring homomorphism onto
$E:=\{(u,v)\in\Z\times\Z : u\equiv v \bmod 2\}$, a subring of $\Z\times\Z$ with componentwise
operations, and $\Phi^{-1}(u,v)=\tfrac{u+v}2+\tfrac{u-v}2 t$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\Phi$ is a ring map since $\chi_\pm$ are; $(a+b)-(a-b)=2b$ is even so the image lies in $E$;
conversely $u\equiv v$ makes $a=(u+v)/2$, $b=(u-v)/2$ integral. Parity arithmetic shows $E$ is
closed under $+,-,\times$ and contains $1=(1,1)$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The twist is a unit, not a prime}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{twist} is $t$, with $\Phi(t)=(1,-1)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:twunit}
$t^2=1$, $t\ne\pm1$, and $t$ is a unit (its own inverse). Consequently $t$ is neither prime nor
irreducible.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Holonomy]\label{thm:holonomy}
For $n\in\mathbb{N}$, $t^n=1$ if $n$ is even and $t^n=t$ if $n$ is odd; the homomorphism
$n\mapsto t^n$ has kernel $2\Z$, matching $\sigma^2=\mathrm{id}$ for the deck transformation
$\sigma(x,y)=(x+1,-y)$ of the orientation double cover.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{The norm and the unit group}

\begin{definition}
$N(a+bt):=\chi_+(a+bt)\chi_-(a+bt)=(a+b)(a-b)=a^2-b^2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Multiplicativity]
$N(zw)=N(z)N(w)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
In the model $N$ is the product of the two coordinates and multiplication is componentwise.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Units]\label{thm:units}
$z$ is a unit iff $N(z)=\pm1$, and $\Ztw^\times=\{1,-1,t,-t\}\cong(\Z/2)^2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$zw=1$ gives $N(z)N(w)=1$. Conversely if the coordinates $(u,v)$ of $z$ satisfy $uv=\pm1$ then
$u,v\in\{\pm1\}$, so $z^2=(1,1)=1$. In $a+bt$ coordinates $(a+b)(a-b)=\pm1$ forces
$(a,b)\in\{(\pm1,0),(0,\pm1)\}$; every element squares to $1$, so the group is elementary
abelian of order $4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The sign $-1$ and the twist $t$ are independent units: e.g. $6t\ne\pm6$, since $\Phi(6t)=(6,-6)$
while $\Phi(\pm6)=\pm(6,6)$. The twist is not the sign.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Zero divisors: the seam, algebraically}

\begin{theorem}
$(1+t)(1-t)=1-t^2=0$ with $1\pm t\ne0$; hence $\Ztw$ is not an integral domain.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Zero-divisor locus]\label{thm:zd}
For $z=a+bt$ the following are equivalent: (i) $zw=0$ for some $w\ne0$; (ii) $N(z)=0$;
(iii) $a=b$ or $a=-b$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
In coordinates $z=(u,v)$ and $zw=(uw_1,vw_2)$. If $u=0$ then $z(1+t)=0$ with $1+t\mapsto(2,0)\ne
0$; if $v=0$ then $z(1-t)=0$. Conversely if $zw=0$ with $w\ne0$, some coordinate of $w$ is
nonzero and the matching coordinate of $z$ vanishes, so $N(z)=uv=0$. Finally
$uv=a^2-b^2=0\iff a=\pm b$.
\end{proof}

The locus is a union of two lines through the origin --- the algebraic image of the gluing
$(0,y)\sim(1,-y)$, which likewise glues each fibre to its reflection.

\subsection{The parity obstruction and irreducibility}

\begin{theorem}[No norm $\pm2$]\label{thm:notwo}
$N(z)\notin\{2,-2\}$ for every $z\in\Ztw$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $\Phi(z)=(u,v)$ with $u\equiv v \bmod 2$. If both are odd, $uv$ is odd; if both are even,
$4\mid uv$. Neither allows $|uv|=2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Prime norm implies irreducible]\label{thm:primenorm}
If $|N(z)|$ is a rational prime then $z$ is irreducible.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$z$ is not a unit as $|N(z)|\ne1$; if $z=xy$ then $|N(x)||N(y)|$ is prime, so one factor has
$|N|=1$ and is a unit by Theorem~\ref{thm:units}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Norm four implies irreducible]\label{thm:normfour}
If $N(z)=4$ then $z$ is irreducible.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $z=xy$ then $|N(x)||N(y)|=4$ with neither factor equal to $2$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:notwo}, so
one factor is $1$ and the corresponding element is a unit.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\pm2$ are irreducible ($N=4$), and $2\pm t$ are irreducible ($N=3$).
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Every odd integer splits]\label{thm:oddsplit}
For every $k\in\Z$, $2k+1=\big((k+1)+kt\big)\big((k+1)-kt\big)$, and for $k\notin\{0,-1\}$
neither factor is a unit.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$(k+1)^2-k^2=2k+1$ and the $t$-coefficient cancels. Non-unitness follows from the explicit unit
list: $(k+1,\pm k)\in\{(\pm1,0),(0,\pm1)\}$ only for $k\in\{0,-1\}$.
\end{proof}

For instance $3=(2+t)(2-t)$, $5=(3+2t)(3-2t)$, $7=(4+3t)(4-3t)$.

\begin{theorem}[Classification of irreducible rational integers]\label{thm:classify}
For $n\in\Z$, the element $n\in\Ztw$ is irreducible if and only if $n=\pm2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Leftarrow$) Theorem~\ref{thm:normfour}. ($\Rightarrow$) If $n=2m$ with $m=0$ then $n=0$ is
reducible ($0=(1+t)(1-t)$, and $0$ is never irreducible); if $m=\pm1$ then $n=\pm2$; otherwise
$n=2\cdot m$ with both factors non-units. If $n$ is odd, $\pm1$ are units and every other odd
$n=2k+1$ splits nontrivially by Theorem~\ref{thm:oddsplit}.
\end{proof}

Thus the twist ring inverts intuition: the only irreducible rational integer is $2$, the unique
prime that the hyperbolic form $a^2-b^2$ cannot represent, by the parity obstruction.

\subsection{The factorisation test, answered}

\begin{theorem}[The three test cases]\label{thm:test}
\begin{enumerate}
\item $6=2\,(2+t)(2-t)$ with all three factors irreducible: $6$ has \emph{three} irreducible
factors, not two.
\item $-6=(-1)\cdot 2\,(2+t)(2-t)$, where $-1$ is a \emph{unit}, not a prime; and $-1\ne t$, so
the sign is not the twist. Moreover $6t\notin\{6,-6\}$.
\item $0=(1+t)(1-t)$ with both factors nonzero: even zero factors nontrivially, which is
impossible in $\Z$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

This is the corrected form of (C5): the ``overall sign'' of a factorisation is a unit, as in
$\Z$, but the unit group is the Klein four-group $\{\pm1,\pm t\}$ whose extra generator is the
orientation label. Since $\Ztw$ is not a domain it is not a UFD, but
Theorem~\ref{thm:classify} describes the rational-integer locus completely.

\subsection{Nonsplitness: no nontrivial idempotents}

Over $\Q$ the relation $t^2=1$ splits the ring: $e_\pm=\tfrac12(1\pm t)$ are orthogonal
idempotents and $\Q[t]/(t^2-1)\cong\Q\times\Q$. Over $\Z$ the halving is unavailable, and this
is not a technicality.

\begin{theorem}[Idempotent rigidity]\label{thm:idem}
If $e\in\Ztw$ satisfies $e^2=e$ then $e\in\{0,1\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
In coordinates $e=(u,v)$ with $u\equiv v \bmod 2$ and $u^2=u$, $v^2=v$, so $u,v\in\{0,1\}$;
parity rules out $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Nonsplitting]
$\Ztw\not\cong\Z\times\Z$ as rings.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\Z\times\Z$ has the idempotent $(1,0)\notin\{0,1\}$, and ring isomorphisms preserve idempotents
and $0,1$; this contradicts Theorem~\ref{thm:idem}.
\end{proof}

The congruence $u\equiv v \bmod 2$ is an integral gluing datum: the orientation eigenspaces
cannot be separated over $\Z$, only after inverting $2$. This is the arithmetic fingerprint of
non-orientability, and it is the same $2$ that obstructs norm values $\pm2$.

\section{Sections of the M\"obius line bundle}\label{sec:sections}

\begin{definition}
$f:\R\to\R$ is \emph{periodic} if $f(x+1)=f(x)$ and \emph{antiperiodic} if $f(x+1)=-f(x)$.
Write $P$ and $A$ for the two sets. Periodic functions are sections of the trivial line bundle
over $\R/\Z$; antiperiodic functions are sections of the M\"obius line bundle.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Grading rules]\label{thm:grading}
$P\cdot P\subseteq P$, $P\cdot A\subseteq A$, $A\cdot A\subseteq P$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $f,g\in A$ then $f(x+1)g(x+1)=(-f)(-g)=fg$; a single antiperiodic factor contributes one
sign; two periodic factors contribute none.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Directness]
$P\cap A=\{0\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$f(x)=f(x+1)=-f(x)$ gives $2f(x)=0$.
\end{proof}

Thus $P\oplus A$ is a $\Z/2$-graded multiplicative system whose grading group is $\{1,t\}$; its
group ring is exactly $\Ztw$, so Theorems~\ref{thm:twunit} and \ref{thm:grading} are the same
statement in two languages.

\begin{theorem}[The odd part is not a ring]
$A$ is not closed under multiplication: $f(x)=\cos(\pi x)$ is antiperiodic but $f^2$ is not.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\cos(\pi(x+1))=-\cos(\pi x)$. If $f^2$ were antiperiodic, evaluating at $x=0$ would give
$\cos^2\pi=-\cos^2 0$, i.e. $1=-1$.
\end{proof}

Hence M\"obius sections form a module over the periodic functions, never an algebra --- an
intrinsic reason why (C2) had to fail.

\begin{theorem}[Shift law and bridge]\label{thm:shift}
If $f\in A$ then $f(x+n)=(-1)^n f(x)$ for all $n\in\mathbb{N}$; equivalently
$f(x+n)=\varepsilon_n f(x)$ with $\varepsilon_n=+1$ exactly when $t^n=1$ in $\Ztw$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$ for the first claim; then Theorem~\ref{thm:holonomy}, since $t^n=1$ iff $n$ is
even iff $(-1)^n=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A zero in every window]\label{thm:zero}
Let $f$ be continuous and antiperiodic. Then for every $a\in\R$ there is $x\in[a,a+1]$ with
$f(x)=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$f(a+1)=-f(a)$, so $0$ lies between $f(a)$ and $f(a+1)$; apply the intermediate value theorem
on $[a,a+1]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
There is a zero in $[0,1]$; the zero set is unbounded; and for every $n\in\mathbb{N}$ each
window $[k,k+1]\subseteq[0,n]$ contains a zero, so $[0,n]$ contains at least $n$ zeros.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[No nowhere-vanishing section; no units in the odd part]
There is no continuous $f$ with $f(x+1)=-f(x)$ and $f(x)\ne0$ for all $x$; hence the M\"obius
line bundle is nontrivial. Consequently no continuous antiperiodic $f$ admits $g$ with
$fg\equiv1$, while the even part does contain units (e.g. the constant $1$): all units live in
degree $0$.
\end{theorem}

This is the analytic counterpart of Theorem~\ref{thm:twunit}: in the twist ring the orientation
class \emph{is} a unit, hence not prime; in the ring of continuous functions the odd part
contains \emph{no} units. Both say that orientation is a grading.

\section{Algorithms}\label{sec:algo}

\paragraph{Arithmetic and norm.} With $(a,b)$-coordinates,
$(a,b)+(c,d)=(a+c,b+d)$ and $(a,b)(c,d)=(ac+bd,ad+bc)$, each $O(1)$ integer operations; the
norm $N(a,b)=(a+b)(a-b)$ costs one multiplication.

\paragraph{Unit and zero-divisor tests.} $z$ is a unit iff $N(z)=\pm1$ and a zero divisor iff
$N(z)=0$. When $N(z)=0$, an explicit annihilator is $1+t$ if $\chi_+(z)=0$, and $1-t$ if
$\chi_-(z)=0$.

\paragraph{Factorisation of a rational integer $n$.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $n=0$, return $(1+t)(1-t)$.
\item If $n=\pm1$, return the unit $n$.
\item Write $n=\pm2^e m$ with $m>0$ odd; emit the sign as a unit and $e$ copies of $2$.
\item Factor $m=p_1^{a_1}\cdots p_k^{a_k}$ over $\Z$ (trial division: $O(\sqrt m)$).
\item Replace each odd prime $p=2k_p+1$ by $\big((k_p+1)+k_pt\big)\big((k_p+1)-k_pt\big)$, each
irreducible by Theorem~\ref{thm:primenorm}.
\end{enumerate}
The number of irreducible factors is $e+2\Omega(m)$: odd primes double, the prime $2$ does not.
For $n=6$ this yields $2\cdot(2+t)\cdot(2-t)$.

\paragraph{Locating forced zeros.} For continuous antiperiodic $f$ on $[a,a+1]$, bisection keeps
the subinterval whose endpoint values have opposite signs (one always does, since
$f(a+1)=-f(a)$), localising a zero to width $2^{-k}$ after $k$ evaluations --- a constructive
form of Theorem~\ref{thm:zero}.

\section{Applications and interpretation}\label{sec:apps}

\paragraph{Orientation as a grading.} Whenever a finite-order geometric symmetry is imported
into algebra it appears in the \emph{unit group} and induces a grading, not among the
irreducibles: a $G$-grading is a coaction of $\Z[G]$, and $G\subseteq\Z[G]^\times$. Searching
for a ``twist prime'' is a category error that the twist ring makes visible.

\paragraph{A calibrated non-domain.} $\Ztw$ is a small, fully explicit commutative ring with
zero divisors, four units, a multiplicative norm and a complete classification of irreducible
rational integers --- a useful test case for factorisation outside integral domains, where even
$0$ factors nontrivially and $2$ is the only irreducible rational integer.

\paragraph{Signal processing.} Antiperiodic signals (half-wave symmetry, odd-harmonics-only
waveforms) must cross zero at least once per period by Theorem~\ref{thm:zero}, a model-free
guarantee; and products of two odd-parity signals are even by Theorem~\ref{thm:grading}. The
twist ring is the bookkeeping device for that parity, with $N=a^2-b^2$ the product of the
even-channel and odd-channel amplitudes.

\paragraph{Two-channel decompositions in learning systems.} Constructions splitting data into
symmetric and antisymmetric channels --- even/odd features, sign-equivariant parameterisations,
$\pm1$ gauge variables --- are $\Ztw$-modules. Theorem~\ref{thm:idem} warns that the two
channels cannot be separated \emph{integrally}: eigenchannel decomposition requires dividing by
$2$, so integer-constrained implementations retain a genuine coupling; Theorem~\ref{thm:zero} is
the analytic version, since an antisymmetric response cannot be bounded away from zero.

\paragraph{Physics analogy, corrected.} Orientation resembles spin in that both are $\Z/2$
labels realised by an order-two symmetry acting by units, and both need two laps to return. It
differs in that spin is not a constituent --- and neither is orientation.

\section{Discussion and limitations}

Theorems~\ref{thm:noadd} and \ref{thm:nomul} refute the existence of any operation compatible
with coordinatewise operations on representatives; they do not say that $M$ admits no algebraic
structure at all --- it supports fibrewise addition of sections and a module structure over the
periodic functions. No reparametrisation rescues the identification claim: gluing $n$ with $-n$
requires opposite fibre coordinates, while $|n|$ makes them equal. Nor does one rescue the
zero-divisor claim: the seam identifies $(1,0)$ with $(0,0)$ because $-0=0$.

$\Ztw$ is not a UFD --- it is not even a domain --- so Theorem~\ref{thm:classify} should be read
as a classification on the rational-integer locus; a general element with composite norm may
factor in genuinely different ways, and elements on the seam lines are zero divisors. Finally,
the forced-vanishing results are specific to rank one: an antiperiodic map $\R\to\R^d$ with
$d\ge2$ need not vanish, e.g. $x\mapsto(\cos\pi x,\sin\pi x)$.

\section{Future directions}\label{sec:future}

\paragraph{Twisted group-ring factorisation spectrum.} For finite abelian $G$, the multiset of
irreducible factors of a rational prime $p$ in $\Z[G]$ should be computed by the splitting of
$p$ in the ring of integers of each cyclotomic character factor, with a uniform ``norm $\pm2$
obstruction'' generalising Theorem~\ref{thm:notwo}. The key insight is that the form $a^2-b^2$
of $\Z[\Z/2]$ is the field norm glued along the character decomposition, so the local
obstruction becomes a congruence condition on $p$ modulo $\exp(G)$ times a $2$-adic defect.

\paragraph{Idempotent rigidity of non-orientable group rings.} Conjecturally $\Z[G]$ has no
idempotents other than $0,1$ for every finite abelian $G$ of even order, whereas $\Q[G]$ splits
completely; the congruence $u\equiv v \bmod 2$ is an integral gluing datum no idempotent can
respect, exactly as in Theorem~\ref{thm:idem}, and the argument should apply to each pair of
conjugate characters.

\paragraph{Rank threshold for nowhere-vanishing twisted sections.} The scalar case is settled
(Theorem~\ref{thm:zero}). For twisted $\R^d$-bundles over the circle with holonomy an involution
$\sigma\in O(d)$, one expects nowhere-vanishing sections to exist exactly when $\sigma$ fixes a
nonzero vector, with the rank-one M\"obius case ($\sigma=-1$) extremal.

\paragraph{Quantitative zero counting and graded number theory.} For smooth sections one expects
Sturm-type refinements of the ``one zero per window'' bound under antiperiodic boundary
conditions; and more broadly, one can ask what unique factorisation should mean for a
$\Z/2$-graded ring with an integral gluing congruence, with Theorem~\ref{thm:classify} as the
model answer.

\section{Conclusion}

The proposal to found arithmetic on the M\"obius band contains one correct and illuminating idea
--- that $y(2x-1)$ is well defined on the band, i.e. a section of the twisted line bundle ---
surrounded by four false claims: no induced coordinatewise operations exist; the embedding of
$\Z$ records only the sign; it identifies nothing and compactifies nothing; and its proposed
zero divisor has a factor that is already zero.

The intuition, however, lands on the twist ring $\Z[t]/(t^2-1)$: a commutative ring with zero
divisors whose unit group $\{\pm1,\pm t\}$ contains the orientation class, whose norm $a^2-b^2$
obeys a parity obstruction making $2$ the unique irreducible rational integer, in which
$6=2(2+t)(2-t)$, $-6$ is that product times a unit, and $0=(1+t)(1-t)$, and which --- despite
$t^2=1$ --- does not split as $\Z\times\Z$. On the analytic side the same $\Z/2$ grades periodic
and antiperiodic functions, the holonomy is computed by powers of $t$, and the odd part contains
no units because every continuous antiperiodic function vanishes in every unit window.
Orientation is not a prime: it is a unit of order two, and what it generates is a grading.

